{"type":"assistant.message","data":{"messageId":"7641f378-832a-4a8e-9c42-c70fe307d01b","content":"Nice scenario. Here’s a concrete, implementation‑oriented plan for PST support in this app.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Clarify Requirements\n\n- Max PST size and total volume?  \n- Expected latency: offline preprocessing vs “upload and chat immediately”?  \n- Security/compliance: do we need to scrub attachments, signatures, etc.?  \n- Target content: whole emails, or only subset (subject/body, no attachments)?\n\n---\n\n### 2. High-Level Architecture\n\n1. **Upload**: Treat `.pst` like any other file but add `pst` to `ALLOWED_EXTENSIONS`.  \n2. **Extraction pipeline**: For PSTs, run an extraction job that:\n   - Walks folders and messages\n   - Produces normalized email documents (JSON or text)  \n3. **Chunking + Embedding**: Reuse existing document chunking and `generate_embedding` pipeline for each email/body segment.  \n4. **Indexing**: Store each email chunk in Azure AI Search as a regular document with metadata (sender, subject, date, folder, messageId).  \n5. **RAG**: Chat flow stays the same; retrieved chunks now can be “email” chunks.\n\n---\n\n### 3. PST Parsing Technology Choice\n\nPick one and validate in an isolated script first:\n\n- **Python libraries** (most natural for this repo):\n  - `pypff` (libpff bindings) or `extract-msg` (for .msg, but can help if you export).\n- Alternative: **external service** or **container** that:\n  - Accepts PST, returns JSON of emails.\n  - Simple HTTP integration from this app.\n\n**Recommendation:** Start with a separate micro‑script using `pypff` to confirm you can:\n- Enumerate folders\n- Extract `subject`, `body`, `from`, `to`, `cc`, `sent/received date`\n- Handle HTML vs plain text bodies\n\n---\n\n### 4. Data Model & Metadata\n\nDefine a normalized “email document” format (even if just in code):\n\n```json\n{\n  \"conversation_id\": \"<pst-relative or global>\",\n  \"message_id\": \"<unique per email>\",\n  \"folder\": \"Inbox/Subfolder\",\n  \"subject\": \"...\",\n  \"from\": \"name@example.com\",\n  \"to\": [\"...\"],\n  \"cc\": [\"...\"],\n  \"sent_time\": \"ISO8601\",\n  \"received_time\": \"ISO8601\",\n  \"body_text\": \"...\",\n  \"body_html\": \"...\",\n  \"attachments\": [\n    {\"filename\": \"x.pdf\", \"mimetype\": \"application/pdf\", \"size\": 12345}\n  ],\n  \"source_file\": \"original.pst\",\n  \"source_type\": \"pst-email\"\n}\n```\n\nStore this logically in:\n- **Cosmos**: metadata per PST + derived per-email docs (if needed).\n- **Search index**: one record per *chunk* of `body_text` + key metadata.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Integration into Existing Pipeline\n\n1. **Allow PST uploads**\n   - Add `'pst'` to `ALLOWED_EXTENSIONS` in `config.py`.\n   - Ensure size limits are acceptable (PST can be huge; maybe add a per-PST cap).\n\n2. **Branch on file type**\n   - In `functions_documents.py` (or wherever upload handling lives), detect `.pst`.\n   - Create a new processing path: `process_pst_document(...)`.\n\n3. **PST extraction step**\n   - Implement `process_pst_document` to:\n     1. Save PST to temp storage or blob (already done for other files).\n     2. Call `extract_emails_from_pst(file_path)` (new helper module).\n     3. For each email:\n        - Build normalized email object.\n        - Push text into existing chunking pipeline.\n\n4. **Chunking**\n   - Reuse `RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter` (already imported in `config.py`).\n   - For each email, generate chunks from `body_text` (plus maybe subject, key metadata).\n   - Each chunk gets:\n     - `content`: chunk of body\n     - `subject`, `from`, `sent_time`, `folder`, etc., as fields\n     - `source_file`: PST filename\n     - `source_document_id`: the PST document’s ID (for grouping in UI)\n\n5. **Embedding + Indexing**\n   - Reuse `generate_embedding` and `add_file_to_search_index` style functions already used for PDFs/Office docs.\n   - Ensure the search index schema includes fields for:\n     - `subject`, `from`, `to`, `folder`, `sent_time`, `source_type`, `source_file`, `message_id`.\n\n---\n\n### 6. RAG Behavior & UX\n\n1. **Retrieval**\n   - No big changes: query Azure Search as you do today.\n   - When results come from `source_type == \"pst-email\"`, display them as emails:\n     - Subject, sender, date, folder snippet.\n     - Chunk text as the “email body”.\n\n2. **Filtering controls (optional but nice)**\n   - Add filters in search:\n     - Date range\n     - Sender/recipient\n     - Folder\n   - Use Search filters (OData) to implement.\n\n3. **UI state for large PSTs**\n   - PSTs may take a while to process:\n     - Show “indexing in progress” status.\n     - Display progress like “Processed 1,254 of 10,000 messages”.\n\n---\n\n### 7. Operational & Performance Considerations\n\n- **Large PSTs**:\n  - Set a max message limit per PST or per user.\n  - Consider streaming processing + periodic commits to Cosmos/Search.\n- **Timeouts & async processing**:\n  - Offload PST processing to an async executor (you already use `flask_executor.Executor`).\n  - Have upload return quickly with a document ID and a background job performing extraction and indexing.\n- **Resilience**:\n  - Log progress to your existing file processing log (`add_file_task_to_file_processing_log`).\n  - On crash, support resuming PST processing from last processed message index.\n\n---\n\n### 8. Security & Compliance\n\n- Ensure PSTs are only accessible to the uploading user/group/workspace (re-use existing Cosmos partitioning and access controls).\n- Decide whether to:\n  - Index attachment content at all (maybe skip for first version).\n  - Strip out email signatures / disclaimers (optional text cleanup step).\n- Consider PII: you likely want this RAG only for the tenant’s users; confirm that.\n\n---\n\n### 9. Testing Plan\n\n1. **Unit/Component Tests**\n   - For `extract_emails_from_pst` using a small test PST:\n     - Validate folder traversal.\n     - Validate correct parsing of subjects, from/to, body.\n\n2. **Functional tests (in `functional_tests/`)**\n   - Upload a small PST (few dozen emails).\n   - Wait for processing to finish.\n   - Ask a chat question that should be answered from a known email.\n   - Validate that:\n     - The response cites email chunks.\n     - Metadata (subject/sender/date) appears correctly in UI.\n\n3. **Edge Cases**\n   - PST with only calendar/tasks (skip gracefully).\n   - Bad / corrupt PST (log and mark document as failed with a clear status).\n   - PST with mixed HTML/plain emails.\n\n---\n\n### 10. Rollout Phases\n\n1. **Phase 1: Prototype**\n   - Local script to convert PST → normalized JSON emails.\n   - Manual call into existing chunking + indexing.\n\n2. **Phase 2: Wire into upload pipeline**\n   - Add `.pst` extension, new processing function, background job.\n   - Minimal UI: just treat as any other document, but with email-style source display.\n\n3. **Phase 3: UX + Filters**\n   - Add “Email source” filters and nice formatting for citations.\n   - Optimize for large PST performance (batch indexing, chunk size tuning).\n\n---\n\nIf you’d like, next step I can:  \n- Propose a concrete `extract_emails_from_pst` Python skeleton, and  \n- Sketch how to plug it into `functions_documents.py` using your existing patterns.","toolRequests":[],"interactionId":"52895768-04cf-4e67-89e8-6a126ce68c6e","outputTokens":1726},"id":"df05e37d-35e4-4e16-9347-0d0ceda5b956","timestamp":"2026-04-16T11:53:50.921Z","parentId":"48055955-7f89-459d-9b33-cc35dcfb4f2a"}
